        "raw": "<p>So, a couple of things that might help your villains out against the Hinder/Wicked solo centric combo.&nbsp;</p>\r\n\r\n<p>1. In order to create a loop where they massively hinder your villain, they aren't going to be very focused on attack.&nbsp; Wicked solo is a green ability, and isn't super strong (since it uses the mid die).&nbsp; In order to get a loop where the villain is at around -10, pretty much every member of the team has to be focused strictly on hindering (with some light attacks).&nbsp; It's a strong play on their part, but by no means unstoppable.&nbsp;</p>\r\n\r\n<p>2. Environmental challenges, minions, and distractions&nbsp;are your best friend here. As soon as they start having things go wrong, the team is going to have to stop their hinders (though muse's is persistent), and that makes Rockstar's wicked solo a lot scarier. Stolen Legacy and Urban Infestation have a lot of suggested ones.&nbsp; I tend to prefer the ones that can be escalated to later yellow/red scenarios (a cracked gas line can lead to a later fire, even if it is temporarily repaired, or a building that has had people knocked into it can fall down).&nbsp; Minions can swarm the weaker members (especially muse, as she is light on HP) (Myriad's swarm is capable of some truly appalling damage).&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;For scene twists, one of the things I've found to be particularly effective in getting player's attention is when you degrade one of their dice for the rest of the fight, until they take the time to fix whatever is screwing with their powers. &nbsp;</p>\r\n\r\n<p>3. The Scene Tracker: If the players are dragging their heels, ignoring challenges, and focusing on locking down a villain... let them.&nbsp; You'll get your vengeance when the tracker runs out, the villain flys off,&nbsp;and they are left with a ruined park and evereyone screaming for their heads.&nbsp; &nbsp;</p>\r\n\r\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\r\n\r\n<p>On the whole, the point is to make sure that they